## std::async_io — async standard-stream I/O (AsyncStdin slice).
##
## Spec: docs/prompts/v1/15_phase4_async.md DoD bullet 4 ("Async*
## variants for the 9 stdlib types"). AsyncStdin is the single new
## type shipped here; AsyncStdout / AsyncStderr are deferred to v1.1
## with a rationale documented in `Riven.toml` (kernel write-buffering
## makes non-blocking stdout/stderr near-useless in v1; blocking
## writes via std::io cover the demand profile).
##
## Surface:
##
##   AsyncStdin.read_line() -> AsyncReadLineFuture
##       Reads from fd 0 until '\n' or EOF. Returns
##       Result[String, IoError]. Newline character is INCLUDED in the
##       returned string (matches Rust's BufRead::read_line). Empty
##       string indicates EOF before any bytes were read.
##
## Why static-method-only (no `AsyncStdin` instance constructor):
##   stdin's fd is process-global. Letting users `var s = AsyncStdin.new`
##   would invite the question "what does it mean to have two stdin
##   handles?" — the answer is "they share the same fd and you'll get
##   interleaved reads", which is not a useful semantic. We surface
##   only the static method so the question doesn't arise.
##
## Concurrency note: in v1 the executor is single-threaded, so even if
## two tasks each call AsyncStdin.read_line concurrently their poll
## bodies serialize. They'll still interleave bytes (each poll cycle
## reads one byte), so this isn't a recommended pattern — but it
## doesn't UAF either. v2's multi-threaded scheduler will need a
## mutex around the read path.

## --- Reactor fd-readiness primitives (re-declared so this package
## stands on its own, mirroring async_fs/async_net). These are the
## same C symbols already declared in async_fs/src/lib.rvn — Riven's
## FFI registration is keyed on the (symbol, ABI) pair, so identical
## decls in two packages refer to the same linker symbol.
lib "../../future/runtime/reactor.c"
  def riven_reactor_register_fd_read(reactor: Int, fd: Int) -> Int
end

## --- async_stdin state-machine primitives ------------------------------
##
## See library/std/async_io/runtime/async_io.c for the C side. The state
## struct owns its own line buffer + saves/restores fd 0's fcntl flags.
lib "runtime/async_io.c"
  def riven_async_stdin_state_new() -> Int
  def riven_async_stdin_state_get_fd(state: Int) -> Int
  def riven_async_stdin_step(state: Int) -> Int
  def riven_async_stdin_state_take_result(state: Int) -> Result[String, IoError]
  def riven_async_stdin_state_free(state: Int) -> ()
end
